= Thought suppression — Technical =

== Paradigm structure ==
A white-bear-style paradigm can include:

<pre>
baseline / monitoring
        ↓
suppression period
        ↓
post-suppression expression or monitoring
</pre>

Immediate target-thought frequency and post-suppression rebound are distinct outcomes.

== Measurement ==
Measures include self-reported intrusions, verbal reports, response latency, lexical accessibility, electrophysiology, and memory performance.

Self-report is vulnerable to demand characteristics and reporting compliance.

== Cognitive load ==
Classic ironic-process predictions expect suppression to become more fragile under load because controlled replacement processes require capacity while monitoring remains active.

Evidence for this interaction is stronger than a blanket claim that suppression always increases target thoughts.

== White-bear versus think/no-think ==
Think/no-think studies often report suppression-induced forgetting for learned cue–target memories.

These results should not be treated as direct replication or refutation of white-bear thought-rebound effects because the paradigms manipulate different cognitive operations.

== Current evidence ==
Recent review work argues that healthy participants can often suppress thoughts and that evidence for universal paradoxical rebound is insufficient.

The state of evidence therefore supports moderator-sensitive rather than categorical conclusions.

== References ==
* Wegner (1994), ironic processes.
* Wenzlaff & Wegner review.
